# Copy this file to .env and edit it:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Compose reads .env twice over: once to fill in the ${...} placeholders in
# compose.yaml, and again — through `env_file` — to hand these variables to the
# process inside the container. So one file configures both the stack and the
# application.
#
# Everything here is commented out, and that is the point. A commented-out line
# means "I did not set this", and KilasFlow then uses its own default. A line set
# to an empty value means something different and usually worse: an empty
# KILASFLOW_DATABASE_DRIVER, for instance, fails validation at boot with
# `database.driver "" must be sqlite or postgres`, while an absent one correctly
# falls back to SQLite. Uncomment what you mean to change; leave the rest alone.
#
# Values are read literally, so do not wrap them in quotes unless the quotes are
# part of the value. `$` in a value is interpolation and has to be doubled as
# `$$`, and `#` starts a comment — worth knowing if you paste a generated
# password in here. The generated keys below are base64, whose alphabet contains
# neither character, so they are always safe to paste as they are.
#
# The full variable reference — every section, not only the ones a container
# stack cares about — is config.example.yaml. Environment beats config file
# beats default, and only the first underscore after KILASFLOW_ separates the
# section from the key: KILASFLOW_SERVER_READ_HEADER_TIMEOUT sets
# server.read_header_timeout, not server.read.header.timeout.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which image to run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# No KilasFlow release has been published yet. The release workflow and the
# multi-architecture build both exist, but no version tag has ever been pushed,
# so there is nothing in ghcr.io to pull and the default is a locally built
# image. Build it once with:
#
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.build.yaml up -d --build
#
# When the first release lands, pin the exact version instead and drop the build
# overlay — a pull is seconds where a build is minutes:
#
#   KILASFLOW_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kilaslab/kilasflow:v1.2.3
#
# Pin the full vX.Y.Z. The vX.Y and latest tags both move onto later releases, so
# a stack tracking either can change on a restart you did not intend to be an
# upgrade. scripts/docker-tags.sh documents what each tag promises.
#KILASFLOW_IMAGE=kilasflow:local

# Host port for the editor and the API. The container always listens on 8080;
# this only moves the outside of the mapping, which is worth doing when something
# else on this machine already owns 8080.
#KILASFLOW_HOST_PORT=8080


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keys
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All three are absent by default, and KilasFlow starts anyway. That is a
# deliberate choice rather than an oversight: refusing to boot without them would
# make three secrets mandatory for somebody who only wants to look at the editor.
# The cost is that the capability each key unlocks is silently off until you set
# it, so each one below says what you lose by leaving it commented out, and the
# server says the same thing in its log at every start.

# Credential encryption. Without it the server starts and runs workflows, but
# credential storage is disabled: creating a credential fails, and any node that
# needs one cannot run. You will see this line at every boot until it is set:
#
#   level=WARN msg="credential encryption key is not set; credential storage is
#   disabled" variable=KILASFLOW_ENCRYPTION_KEY
#
# Generate one with:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Set it before storing anything. The key encrypts credentials at rest with
# AES-256-GCM, so changing it later makes every credential already stored
# undecryptable — there is no re-encryption pass. Back it up somewhere other than
# this file.
#KILASFLOW_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Embedded editor sessions. Without it, embedding is off: the embed session
# endpoints report that they are unconfigured and the middleware refuses every
# token, so the editor cannot be framed by a host application. Leave it alone
# unless you are embedding KilasFlow in your own product — see the embedding
# guide — and note that embedding also needs an origin allowlist, which is
# config-file territory rather than a single variable.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Deliberately a different key from the two others. One key signing both embed
# tokens and sessions would mean a forged value of either kind could be presented
# as the other.
#KILASFLOW_EMBED_SIGNING_KEY=

# Session and stream-ticket signing, for the authentication section below.
# Generate it the same way, and read that section before turning anything on.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#KILASFLOW_AUTH_SIGNING_KEY=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off by default, and the default is right for a first run. With it off, anyone
# who can reach the port owns the installation — so an instance published beyond
# your own machine needs either this section or something in front of it that
# authenticates. The server says so at every boot:
#
#   level=WARN msg="the API is unauthenticated: anyone who can reach this port
#   owns the installation" enable_with=KILASFLOW_AUTH_ENABLED=true
#
# Turn it on and the first account has to exist, or you have locked yourself out
# of your own server. So uncomment all four lines below together, on the same
# start: the first three create the account, and the fourth begins requiring it.
# KilasFlow refuses to start with authentication on and no signing key, rather
# than coming up and answering every request with 401.
#
# The account is created only on an installation that has none, so these can stay
# in .env across restarts without ever handing a second owner to a deployment
# that already has users. Once you have logged in, remove the password line
# anyway — a password sitting in a file is a password.
#
#KILASFLOW_AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=you@example.com
#KILASFLOW_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=change-me-before-you-use-this
#KILASFLOW_AUTH_SIGNING_KEY=
#KILASFLOW_AUTH_ENABLED=true

# The tenant the first account and all existing data belong to. Leave it alone
# unless you know you want another name: it defaults to `default`, which is the
# tenant rows created before authentication existed are already under, so
# changing it on an existing installation hides that data behind a tenant nobody
# is a member of.
#KILASFLOW_AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_TENANT=default

# Drop the Secure attribute and the __Host- prefix from the session cookie.
#
# Needed only when you sign in over plain HTTP — including http://localhost,
# which browsers otherwise treat as insecure for a __Host- cookie, so a login
# over http:// silently fails to stick without this. Never set it on anything
# reachable over a network: it makes the session cookie readable by anyone on the
# path and plantable by a sibling host, which is exactly what the prefix prevents.
#KILASFLOW_AUTH_COOKIE_INSECURE=true


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL tier
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only read when you add the overlay:
#
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.postgres.yaml up -d
#
# These three configure the postgres service and are woven into the connection
# string the application gets, so changing one changes both ends at once. The
# connection string itself is set by the overlay and is deliberately not a
# variable here — a stale DSN left in .env would follow the default SQLite stack
# around and break it.
#
# Change the password before this is reachable by anything but you. The database
# port is not published to the host, so the default is contained rather than
# safe.
#KILASFLOW_POSTGRES_USER=kilasflow
#KILASFLOW_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=kilasflow
#KILASFLOW_POSTGRES_DB=kilasflow


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Odds and ends
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The image logs JSON, which is what a log shipper wants and not what a person
# reading `docker compose logs` on their first afternoon wants. `text` is easier
# to read; switch back before this matters to anyone else.
#KILASFLOW_LOG_FORMAT=text

# debug | info | warn | error
#KILASFLOW_LOG_LEVEL=info

# How this instance is reachable from the internet, without a trailing slash.
# Empty by default, which disables the self-registration a Telegram or WAHA
# trigger performs — because a bot registered against the wrong address receives
# nothing and reports success. Set it once the instance has a real address.
#KILASFLOW_SERVER_PUBLIC_URL=https://flows.example.com
